hello everyone  welcome back i hope you had fun going  through this uh three notebooks  and just before we leave i'd like to  give you a few references  that are awfully important and useful  for you going forward with the pilots  first of all i already mentioned if  you're interested and want to start  using pilots i strongly recommend to  check out our main documentation  and more importantly we have seen very  briefly how you can create a new  operator and  that has been fun but if you really want  to start  creating new operators please check out  the adding section in our documentation  and  understand all the details that we have  now been able to go through today  and that hopefully will get you started  and able to create  any type and complex type of operator  moreover if you are even more interested  on the  implementation details and want to know  more about the history of pilots  please check out our paper  that we have published a few a year ago  or so  on software x which is freely available  moreover uh we have seen just a very  brief  uh appetizer or what pylops can do  going forward there are two main  locations where you will find  a lot of useful information and useful  examples our tutorial page in the main  documentation  and also here uh this is where i  personally store  most of the notebooks i work on they're  not nice nice and polished as those that  we eventually put in the documentation  but it could be  a good source of inspiration we have  seen that pylops is quite powerful and  useful for  solving problems in different  disciplines and to get the flavor of  different areas of research that are  actually using pilots for  performing cutting edge research we are  trying to store  and uh collect all the different  references to papers that have so far  used by laws  it is quite short right now but there  are quite a few that  are going to come out in the next year  or two  with research that we know and different  people have already  been doing using this library and also  another  sorry very interesting uh direction  where pilots is  going is in the field of astronomy for  the  imaging of strong gravitational lenses  and if you are  not really familiar with this discipline  i really recommend you to check out this  great library where pylopse is now  started to be used to facilitate the  solution of inverse problems  for solving uh actually problems in a  different discipline from the one that  the three of us  uh that we have uh presented pilots they  are familiar with  showing the easy extensibility and also  the flexibility of this library  and finally going forward i like to  mention three different areas where we  have been working on  year and there on and off two libraries  that i will call sibling libraries of  pilots the first  first one is called pylops distributed  the second one is a lot called  gpu distributed is basically a port of  most of the operator  to dusk allowing the uh perform  performing inverse problems in a  distributed system of computers  and so extending the capability of  pilots beyond the single machine  and solving some of the bottom export in  terms of computation and memory  environment  we've also explored for quite a while  the possibility of using pie torch as a  back end compared to numpy and that has  led to  quite a few interesting avenues first of  all  we've been able to create something what  we call the torch operator which is a  overload of a torch module on which you  can  feed any pilots operators so you could  make an impellops operator  actually into an autograd graph  of pytorch so that could be useful when  you want to mix  for example deep neural network with  some physics based  uh processes within the same chain of  operators  and finally this is a very new and very  fresh so far  i've been mostly playing it myself in my  fork but if you are interested to  contribute and you want to see how that  looks like just  please reach out and this is the idea of  actually moving into using coupe i  instead of by  torch when it comes to purely importing  pilots functionalities to gpu  kubai is now a very mature library is  very much  uh one-to-one with numpy so he makes  the change of the code base very limited  compared to having to  kind of rebuild from scratch our library  using pytorch and  it is showing quite a lot of speed up in  several problems so it's uh  something that hopefully will very  quickly make it into the main  codebase if you just want to get in  touch with us  feel free to reach on github and write  an issue whether it's to specific aspect  of the code base or if you are solving a  problem and you're getting stuck  we're also both active on our slack page  but also in the  software underground slack community if  you  are into software and into subsurface  problem is a great place where you can  reach us and also discuss with other  scientists about  different problems of the subsurface  related to software development  an adventure you can also reach out  directly to us on email and linkedin  we are friendly and happy to get more  and more people involved in this project  once again i hope you enjoyed it and  i'll see you in the q a session  you

